 AFT sues Education Department over mismanagement of Public Service Loan Forgiveness program
 90,000 public servants didn’t get credit for some months worked
 Nov. 16, 2026 
 • 
 2 minutes to read The Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) program can be a lifesaver for public employees, including nurses, but according to a lawsuit filed by WSNA’s national union , AFT, the U.S. Department of Education’s mismanagement has left 90,000 borrowers in limbo. 
 “Secretary McMahon has flagrantly ignored her responsibility to help teachers, nurses and first responders,” said AFT President Randi Weingarten, who is the lead plaintiff in the class action lawsuit, Weingarten v. McMahon , filed Oct. 30. The U.S. education secretary, Linda McMahon is the named defendant. 
 “This lawsuit demands that McMahon stop stalling and do her job,” Weingarten said. 
 At issue is a buyback program that allows public employees who were placed in deferment or forbearance for several months to get credit for the time worked. Under the buyback program, borrowers who are near completing the required 120 months for student loan forgiveness can retroactively make those payments and get their loan debt forgiven.
 “ 
 This lawsuit demands that McMahon stop stalling and do her job.” 
 — 
 Randi Weingarten, AFT President But the Education Department has been sitting on some 90,000 applications, reviewing them in no particular order and at a snail’s pace. Borrowers are getting little to no information about their applications, which seem to go into a black hole. 
 In addition, the lawsuit says that beginning in August 2026, the Department of Education started “correcting” the counts of PSLF-qualifying months listed on borrowers' accounts, claiming there were “coding errors.” Months of service that had previously been approved as qualifying PSLF months were erased. 
 The AFT has filed eight lawsuits over the past decade aimed at protecting student loan borrowers, several of those in the past two years. 
 “This is our third case in less than two years against the Trump administration, and the AFT will not stop fighting until every teacher, nurse and public worker gets the loan forgiveness they were promised,” Weingarten said .   
 What to do if this is happening to you 
 Join an upcoming AFT student debt clinic to understand your options and protect your path to relief.
 Share your story with AFT to help them fight for you. Your stories show the courts, policymakers, and the public how these failures are affecting working people and their families.
